\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Hypothesis-driven workflow for catalytic XAS. A scientific request specifies structural alternatives that are generated, relaxed, and evaluated by spectroscopy calculations. The calculation agent manages NERSC execution, and AI-ready records connect the outputs to model training and spectral interpretation. The return arrow represents refinement of the next calculation or hypothesis; it does not imply that a closed-loop catalytic discovery experiment has been completed.\relax }}{4}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:framework}{{1}{4}{Hypothesis-driven workflow for catalytic XAS. A scientific request specifies structural alternatives that are generated, relaxed, and evaluated by spectroscopy calculations. The calculation agent manages NERSC execution, and AI-ready records connect the outputs to model training and spectral interpretation. The return arrow represents refinement of the next calculation or hypothesis; it does not imply that a closed-loop catalytic discovery experiment has been completed.\relax }{figure.caption.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Substrate, adsorbate, and adsorption-site models. (a) Left: the Cu--Au substrate interface rendered from representative input coordinates, showing the two metal regions without adsorbates. Right: schematic structures of the adsorbates considered in the sampling plan. One shared element legend applies to both views: Cu, Au, C, O, and H are blue, gold, dark gray, red, and light gray. (b) Exclusive configuration counts, separated into single-species and coadsorbed states, for the full inventory and completed ML dataset. A single-species state can contain several molecules of that species. Two clean surfaces and one CuO reference are omitted from these adsorbate counts. (c) Seven CO adsorption motifs on one schematic Cu--Au surface, with adsorbates and metal atoms in the same oblique projection. Numbered sites and dashed contact lines define atop, bridge, and hollow coordination, including the boundary-spanning bridge and three hollow compositions. These site illustrations are not relaxed structures.\relax }}{5}{figure.caption.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dataset}{{2}{5}{Substrate, adsorbate, and adsorption-site models. (a) Left: the Cu--Au substrate interface rendered from representative input coordinates, showing the two metal regions without adsorbates. Right: schematic structures of the adsorbates considered in the sampling plan. One shared element legend applies to both views: Cu, Au, C, O, and H are blue, gold, dark gray, red, and light gray. (b) Exclusive configuration counts, separated into single-species and coadsorbed states, for the full inventory and completed ML dataset. A single-species state can contain several molecules of that species. Two clean surfaces and one CuO reference are omitted from these adsorbate counts. (c) Seven CO adsorption motifs on one schematic Cu--Au surface, with adsorbates and metal atoms in the same oblique projection. Numbered sites and dashed contact lines define atop, bridge, and hollow coordination, including the boundary-spanning bridge and three hollow compositions. These site illustrations are not relaxed structures.\relax }{figure.caption.2}{}}
